WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:08.209
Autodetect does not always find every relationship, so in this lesson we create one by hand.

2
00:00:08.209 --> 00:00:11.100
In Manage relationships, click New relationship.

3
00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:14.837
From table:

4
00:00:14.837 --> 00:00:15.256
Orders.

5
00:00:15.256 --> 00:00:16.093
To table:

6
00:00:16.093 --> 00:00:16.930
Order Details.

7
00:00:16.930 --> 00:00:21.115
Then click the column that links them in both tables.

8
00:00:21.115 --> 00:00:25.300
Here it is Order ID, and Power BI highlights it.

9
00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:38.609
Power BI suggests one to many.

10
00:00:38.609 --> 00:00:44.696
Each order appears once in the Orders table, but many times in Order Details,

11
00:00:44.696 --> 00:00:47.740
because one order can contain many products.

12
00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:05.791
The cross filter direction is Single, so filters flow from Orders to Order Details.

13
00:01:05.791 --> 00:01:09.100
Make sure Make this relationship active is ticked.

14
00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:26.206
The cardinality list has four options:

15
00:01:26.206 --> 00:01:30.986
many to one, one to one, one to many, and many to many.

16
00:01:30.986 --> 00:01:33.560
Choose the one that matches your data.

17
00:01:39.000 --> 00:01:42.960
Click Save, and the relationship appears in the list as Active.

18
00:01:50.000 --> 00:01:54.000
In Model view, a line now joins Orders and Order Details.

19
00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:58.000
The one and the star at each end show the cardinality.

20
00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:20.363
Now a one to one example.

21
00:02:20.363 --> 00:02:23.909
On this layout we have Customers and South Customers.

22
00:02:23.909 --> 00:02:27.060
Each customer appears only once in each table.

23
00:02:38.000 --> 00:02:43.105
I create a relationship from Customers to South Customers on Customer ID.

24
00:02:43.105 --> 00:02:49.060
Power BI sets it to one to one, with the cross filter direction Both.

